    "turn_text": "Well, okay, you've put a lot down there, Rachel. Let's walk it backwards just a little bit and take it on Seriatim. So first of all, this resolution, it doesn't have any enforcement effect. It's basically a sense of the Congress, if you will, or a sense of the House resolution. that was supposed to just be a statement going on record to condemn the kind of talk and condemn the kind of bigotry that Ilhan Omar has been fomenting and fulminating. It soon became clear that there were so many on the left that wanted to immunize her from any kind of specific condemnation that they were not going to use her name. So then it became a general resolution, feel-good sense of the Congress resolution, against anti-Semitism. But, you know, there's an entire grievance industry in this country. We've spoken about it. You know what I'm talking about, that every group has to have it. have its due and they thought that even that would be too strong against Ilhan Omar and you have to understand Ilhan Omar along with Shida Tlybe and Alexandria Ocasio-Cortez they are the new face and the new energy of the democratic party and they all supported each other on this by the way and they've all defended those other two defended Ilhan Omar on her comments. Um, so, so it was, it was becoming pretty clear that even just a simple resolution condemning anti-Semitism, which should not be hard. I mean, you know, it should",
