    "turn_text": "one of the oldest forms of bigotry because humans being imperfect you know imperfect um... the idea of of needing someone to blame or having escaped code if you will It's practically inherent in any failed society or any failed community. You need someone to blame for your failures. There's no failed community wants to be introspective. No failed community wants to look to itself for its own problems. And one of the most interesting laws in Judaism that I've always loved, this is just an interesting hour, my gosh. One of the most interesting laws in Judaism I've always loved is the law that says in ancient times if someone was killed in a community. It was obligatory. It was obligatory of the rabbis, the leaders, the religious leaders of the community, to come forward and proclaim that they had nothing to do with that death, that they are not responsible, that the blood was not on their hands, if you will. And there's an interesting, Talmudic question on this, saying why would anyone think in the first place that a rabbi would have been responsible for killing someone. And the answer is, no one would really think that. The obligation is on the rabbis, though, in saying that, that they did everything they could to make sure that someone did not die for an unjust purpose, that it was obligatory on the rabbis, the elders of the community, the learned members of the community, to keep the community safe. And to the degree that we've wandered from that is sad. But to the degree that Jews have been the historically most hated minority in the world, Um, maybe, maybe that's just, you know, it's just the way the world is. You know, there's sometimes there's just no real good answer for it. You're right. At the end of the day, though, Steve, which is why we love America so much, a country that gives to bigotry, no sanction and to persecution, no assistance in George Washington's wonderful phrase. is that this was a country that was supposed to protect all communities of faith, and any faith. As Thomas Jefferson said for my neighbor to say, he prays to one God or 20 gods, neither picks my pocket nor breaks my leg. That's our ethic. All we wanted to do was to be the best Americans there were, and all the Jews wanted to do in this world was to be the best in any community that they existed in. And it turned out that hatred was so extreme and so insidious that after World War II, it became evident that could not happen, at least not in Europe, at least not in Europe. And so, hence the modern Zionist movement, which said we're going to have one country, where we know we will always be safe. And in building that country, we will give equal political rights to any member of any community, which is why the Arabs are elected to the Knesset and have political and voting rights, which is why Arabs serve on the Supreme Court of Israel. It's really a modern marvel. And it's a marvel of success economically, politically, culturally, in every other way, minority rights. And you're not going to find it in any of the 22 Arab countries. So why do you think they would hate it? It kind of answers itself. And it's a sad story.",
